25.5  Configuring git

TL;DR
  - Three levels: system (/etc/gitconfig), yours (~/.gitconfig) and the
    repository's (.git/config). The closest one wins.
  - Set your name and email once; commits carry them forever.
  - .gitignore lists files git should never track: build output, secrets, editor
    files.

      git config --global user.name "Alice Example"
      git config --global user.email alice@example.com
      git config --list --show-origin     # every setting, and its file

On your machine
  Files     /etc/gitconfig, ~/.gitconfig
  Identity  name and email set
  Settings  init.defaultbranch = main
            pull.rebase = true
            core.autocrlf = input
            credential.helper = store (passwords in plain text in
            ~/.git-credentials)
  Aliases   st, lg
  Includes  1 more file, read under conditions (include, includeIf)

Pitfalls
  - A work and a personal email: [includeIf "gitdir:~/work/"] loads a second
    file for repositories under ~/work/.
  - .gitignore doesn't affect files already committed: git rm --cached FILE
    stops tracking one, keeping it on disk.
  - credential.helper = store keeps tokens in plain text; use your desktop
    keyring (libsecret) or SSH keys instead.
  - On Linux, core.autocrlf should be input or unset; true is for Windows.

Deep dive

  ▾ Which settings are worth setting?

    init.defaultBranch (the first branch's name), pull.rebase (how pull
    integrates), push.autoSetupRemote, merge.conflictstyle = zdiff3,
    rebase.autoStash (stash and restore around a rebase) and fetch.prune (forget
    branches deleted on the remote). Aliases
    (git config --global alias.st "status -sb") shorten what you type daily.

  ▾ Where do personal ignores go?

    Files only you produce, like your editor's, belong in ~/.config/git/ignore,
    read for every repository. The project's .gitignore is for what every
    contributor generates.

Related
  Commands  git
  Paths     ~/.gitconfig, ~/.config/git/config, /etc/gitconfig, .git/config
  See also  idk 25.4 Remotes: fetch, pull and push
  See also  idk 22.1 Where environment variables come from
